  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Ancient Roman concrete gets stronger every time seawater hits it. While modern bridges decay in decades, two-thousand-year-old Roman piers still withstand pounding ocean waves.",
        "visual_query": "ancient roman maritime concrete harbour ruins",
        "visual_fallback": "ancient coastal stone ruins"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Engineers long assumed the Romans just got lucky with volcanic ash. But microscopic analysis revealed seawater was actively growing new minerals inside the structure, filling every emerging fracture.",
        "visual_query": "ancient roman underwater harbour underwater ruins",
        "visual_fallback": "ocean floor rocks"
      },
      {
        "narration": "How did ancient builders force concrete to heal itself? The secret was hidden in tiny white specks scattered through the rock\u2014lumps scientists spent decades dismissing as crude craftsmanship.",
        "visual_query": "microscope close up ancient stone texture",
        "visual_fallback": "cracked rock surface"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In 2023, MIT researchers realized those specks were quicklime mixed at extreme heat. When seawater seeps into a new crack, it dissolves the lime, triggering a reaction that crystallizes and glues the gap shut.",
        "visual_query": "mit laboratory material science research analysis",
        "visual_fallback": "science laboratory researcher"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Roman concrete doesn't resist the environment; it uses nature to repair itself. With modern infrastructure crumbling worldwide, why aren't we building our coastal cities with two-thousand-year-old chemistry?",
        "visual_query": "modern coastal city concrete seawall ocean",
        "visual_fallback": "coastal seawall waves"
      }
    ],
